he knight on f6\n- It disrupts Black's coordination\n- The Black king is already somewhat exposed with limited piece protection\n\nReason: B: c4-f7+ delivers a forcing check that improves my position tactically. The bishop is defended by my knight on f6, so Black cannot simply capture it. This check forces Black's king into a worse position and gives me the initiative to continue pressing the attack. In chess, when you can make a forcing move that improves your position (especially checks that are safe), it's often the right choice.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-f7"}
