\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-a3, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: d4-e5, P: d4-d5, B: d7-c6, B: d7-e6, B: d7-f5, B: d7-c8, B: d7-e8, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1, P: g3-g4, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: \nI'm playing as White in a complex middlegame position. Black's king is on b7, relatively exposed. I have a bishop on d7 that is very active and deep in Black's position. Black's queen is on e5, and there's a knight on f5. My king is on g1, relatively safe. Material appears roughly equal, but positionally I need to look for tactical opportunities.\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **B: d7-e8**: This move attacks nothing immediately but advances the bishop further. Not particularly forcing.\n\n2. **B: d7-c8**: Similar to e8, doesn't create immediate threats.\n\n3. **B: d7-e6**: This centralizes the bishop and controls key squares, potentially eyeing the black king's escape squares and supporting a future attack.\n\n4. **B: d7-c6**: This move directly attacks the black king on b7! The king would be in check and forced to move, giving me initiative.\n\n5. **P: d4-d5**: Advances my passed pawn, but it can likely be blockaded or captured.\n\n6. **N: b1-c3** or **N: b1-d2**: Develops the knight but doesn't create immediate threats.\n\nReason:\n**B: d7-c6** is clearly the strongest move as it gives check to the Black king on b7. In chess, forcing moves like checks are often the most powerful, especially when the enemy king is somewhat exposed. This check will force Black's king to move (likely to a8, a7, b8, or c8), disrupting Black's coordination and giving me the initiative. After the king moves, I can potentially follow up with further attacking moves or improve my position while Black is on the defensive.\n\nAnswer: B: d7-c6"}
